The engineering content is solid and the case-study structure (problem → approach → result) is the right skeleton. The problems are in what fills it.

1. There are almost no numbers. This is the single biggest gap. Every result field describes a shape, not an outcome:

"backed by a system engineered to hold up under concurrent real-time chat and video load" — projects.ts:33

"Engineered to hold up" is a statement of intent. A reader can't tell if it held 50 users or 50,000. Only Save In Gold has a real figure ("tens of thousands of leads"), and it's the most convincing line in the file. You have the data — p95 latency, concurrent sessions, records processed, deploy frequency, cost saved. Put one hard number in each result. That change alone does more than everything else on this list.

2. Four list fields per project say the same thing four times. highlights, technicalHighlights, myContribution, challengesSolved — for Vidnik, "built real-time chat with video calling" appears in all four plus caseStudy.approach. That's ~35 bullets per project. Nobody reads bullet 12. I'd collapse to two: What I built (5 bullets max, your work) and Stack (already there). Fold the interesting parts of challengesSolved into the case study's approach, which is where a technical reader actually looks.

3. links: [] on 7 of 9 projects. "Can I see it?" is the first question anyone asks. If these are under NDA or internal, say that explicitly — an empty space reads as "nothing to show," while "Private — client platform, screenshots only" reads as a real job. The two projects with GitHub links are the only ones a reader can verify.

4. The testimonials are a credibility risk. "A true technical leader," "He brings a senior mindset to every architecture discussion" — the register doesn't sound like a Team Leader writing quickly; it sounds like marketing copy. If they're genuine LinkedIn recommendations, link each one to the recommender's profile and the doubt disappears. If they're paraphrased, either get the real wording or cut them. Unverifiable praise costs more than no praise.

5. Thesaurus verbs on About. "Engineered expansive API architectures," "Orchestrated real-time messaging," "Architected and deployed responsive admin dashboards" — AboutContent.tsx:141-160. Senior engineers write "Built" and "Led." The inflated verb reads as compensating.

6. Missing context that hiring managers screen on: no dates per project, no team size, no role scope. "Architected and developed the full backend" — alone, or leading three people? Both are good answers; the absence invites the less flattering guess.

7. The Vidnik subtitle is a 4-line paragraph (projects.ts:26) but the projects list renders it line-clamp-2. It's written for a slot that truncates it. One sentence, then let the case study carry the rest.

